#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_element_norm

#% text
# `finite_field_element_norm(a)` gives the norm of the element `a` of `F_(p^n)` to the prime field: the product of its conjugates `a a^p ... a^(p^(n-1))`, as an integer from 0 to `p - 1`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The norm is `a^((p^n - 1)/(p - 1))`.

#% item
# The norm is multiplicative and is 0 only for 0. Up to the sign `(-1)^n` it is the constant term of the characteristic polynomial of `a`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(5, 3)

#% cell
finite_field_element_norm(F([1, 2]))
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An element of the prime field has the norm `a^n`:

#% cell
finite_field_element_norm(F(3))
#% output
#= 2

#% text
# Zero is the only element of norm 0:

#% cell
finite_field_element_norm(F(0))
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The norm is multiplicative:

#% cell
a = F(17)

#% cell
b = F(99)

#% cell
finite_field_element_norm(a * b) == mod(finite_field_element_norm(a) * finite_field_element_norm(b), 5)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The norm of the generator of a Conway field is the least primitive root modulo p:

#% cell
[finite_field_element_norm(F([0, 1])), primitive_root(5)]
#% output
#= [2, 2]

#% text
# The norm is the product of the conjugates `a`, `a^5` and `a^25`, taken in the field:

#% cell
F([1, 2]) * F([1, 2])^5 * F([1, 2])^25 == F(finite_field_element_norm(F([1, 2])))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The argument must be a finite field element; anything else stays unevaluated:

#% cell
finite_field_element_norm(2)
#% output
#= finite_field_element_norm(2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `finite_field_element_trace`, `finite_field_element_minimal_polynomial`, `finite_field`
